body: "## Evidence (live, 2026-07-25, 2,022-item workspace)\n\n```\n$ pm next --json | jq -c '.recommended | {id,type,title}'\n{\"id\":\"pm-w7ccmv\",\"type\":\"Milestone\",\"title\":\"Outcome milestone: provable history ...\"}\n\n$ pm list --parent pm-w7ccmv --status all --json | jq .count\n0\n\n$ pm next --json | jq -r '.ready[] | \"\\(.id) \\(.type)\"' | head -5\npm-e02lgu Task\npm-kb5h  Chore\npm-5t33or Feature\npm-ed28wi Issue\npm-9rgaal Milestone      <-- second gate in the ready slice\n```\n\nThe single recommendation an agent is told to work next is an outcome milestone with zero children. Outcome milestones are **gates**: they close when the epics beneath them meet their acceptance criteria, they never carry direct execution, and by construction they have no children to walk into. An agent that follows `pm next`, claims the recommendation, and looks for something to do finds nothing — then either releases it as a no-op or, worse, invents work under a gate.\n\n## Two halves, one root\n\n**Half 1 — gate classes are not held out of the actionable set.** The precedent already exists and shipped: Decision-type items are non-executable by an agent lane, so `pm next` routes them to a dedicated `decision_needed` bucket (6 items today) instead of `recommended`/`ready` (pm-eqk0). Milestone gates are the same class of thing and get no such treatment. The fix is not a Milestone special case — it is a declared, schema-driven answer to \"can an agent lane execute an item of this type/shape\", so extension-contributed and custom types inherit it instead of each needing a patch.\n\n**Half 2 — `summary.containers` counts a bucket the envelope never emits.** `pm next --json` reports `summary.containers: 27`, but the envelope keys are `blocked, decision_needed, filters, held_by_others, now, output_default, ready, recommended, summary, truncation` — there is no `containers` key. An agent can see that 27 items were classified as containers and has no way to ask which. Every other classification bucket in this envelope has a worklist; this one is a dead number, which is the same defect shape as the `activity.activity` decoy key (pm-p3x4).\n\n## Why it matters beyond one bad recommendation\n\n`pm next` is the entry point of the agent loop and the intended input to fleet allocation (pm-3oqjnc). A recommender that hands out non-executable gates wastes an entire agent turn per occurrence, and at fleet scale it distributes the same wasted turn across many agents simultaneously. The strategic-reachability work (pm-bzmeaa) deliberately makes every active item reach an outcome milestone through typed edges — which means gates sit near the top of exactly the paths a relevance-ranked recommender favours, so this gets worse as the graph gets healthier, not better.\n\n## Fix direction\n\nExtend the shared actionability definition (`src/sdk/actionability.ts`, shipped by pm-uxkf/PR#579) with a declared executability dimension alongside the existing blocked-vs-ready dimension, so `next`, `context`, `list-blocked`, and `claim --next` all inherit one answer; surface the held-out sets as real envelope keys with worklists; and keep them reachable through an explicit opt-in flag so no capability is removed."
